  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAs of 930 PM EDT...A few short lines of convection are still \nongoing across the region, although coverage and intensity is\nmuch lower than earlier in the evening. The strongest remaining\nstorms are now entering Schoharie County and may be capable of\nproducing heavy downpours and gusty winds. Will continue to\nmonitor for the potential for damaging winds, but this threat\nshould be lowering over the next few hours, as instability\nwanes due to the loss of daytime heating and the atmosphere\nhaving been worked over from previous showers and thunderstorms. \n\nWith the forcing shifting away, all showers and thunderstorms\nshould be done by midnight or shortly after. With the recent\nrainfall and humid air mass still in place, some patchy fog will\noccur through the late night hours.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThunderstorms this afternoon and evening have produced heavy\nrainfall, especially over Ulster County, where localized totals\nmay have exceeded two inches. Some additional showers and\nthunderstorms are expected over the next few hours, especially\nfor parts of the northern and eastern Catskills. There remains a\nlocalized threat for some additional flooding over the next few\nhours in these areas, but the threat overall will be lowering as\nstorms weaken and start to dissipate towards midnight. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nAfternoon Update: No changes to the current forecast. See\nprevious discussion below for more details. \n\nThe storm's main cold front will be crossing on Friday. A few\nadditional showers or thunderstorms are possible on Friday,\nmainly for southern and eastern areas, but coverage will be\nlower than Thursday. There won't be as much instability and\nshear, so storms won't be able to become severe and probably\nwon't be widespread enough to cause any additional flooding.\nThe threat for additional precipitation will end from west to\neast during the afternoon hours with the passage of the cold\nfront.\n\nOver the weekend, high pressure will allow for dry weather and\nseasonable conditions. Plenty of sunshine is expected on\nSaturday with highs in valley areas in the upper 70s to low 80s.\nDewpoints will be more comfortable compared to recent days, with\nvalues in the 50s to low 60s. It will turn slightly more humid\nand warmer on Sunday, but it looks to stay dry with a partly\nsunny sky and highs reaching the lower to middle 80s.",
